"Digestion starts before you even swallow. Your front teeth cut, and your back teeth grind. Your mouth makes about a liter of saliva every day, which wets the food and starts to turn starch into sugar. That is why a plain cracker begins to taste a little sweet if you chew it for a long time. Your tongue then rolls the food into a soft ball and pushes it to the back of your throat. As you swallow, a small flap closes over your windpipe, so the food goes down the right way.": "1b5b9535e8f7",
