"Think about the lunch you ate today, maybe rice, vegetables, and a piece of fish. Over the next day or two, that lunch will travel through a tube several meters long that runs from your mouth all the way through your body. On the way, it will be crushed, soaked, mixed, and broken into pieces so tiny that they can pass into your blood, which carries them to every cell. This is digestion.": "0804db541885",
"Think about the lunch you ate today, maybe rice, vegetables, and a piece of fish. Over the next day or two, that lunch will travel through a tube several meters long that runs from your mouth all the way through your body. On the way, it will be crushed, soaked, mixed, and broken into pieces so tiny that they can pass into your blood, which carries them to every cell. This is digestion. Digestion starts before you even swallow. Your front teeth cut, and your back teeth grind. Your mouth makes about a liter of saliva every day, which wets the food and starts to turn starch into sugar. That is why a plain cracker begins to taste a little sweet if you chew it for a long time. Your tongue then rolls the food into a soft ball and pushes it to the back of your throat. As you swallow, a small flap closes over your windpipe, so the food goes down the right way. The esophagus is a muscle tube about 25 centimeters long. Rings of muscle squeeze one after another, pushing the food down to the stomach in about ten seconds. Gravity is not needed: astronauts can eat and swallow in space. The stomach is a stretchy, J-shaped bag on the left side of your upper belly. Its walls churn the food for two to four hours and mix it with a strong acid and juices, turning lunch into a thick soup called chyme. A layer of mucus protects the stomach from its own acid. Next comes the small intestine, a narrow tube about 5 meters long, coiled up in the middle of your belly. Here, juices from the pancreas and bile from the liver, stored in the gallbladder, finish breaking the food down. The inside wall is covered with millions of tiny, finger-like bumps called villi, which soak up the nutrients and pass them into the blood. Most of the real work of digestion happens here, over three to five hours. What is left moves into the large intestine. It is wider than the small intestine but much shorter, about 1.5 meters long, and it wraps around the small intestine like a frame. Its main job is to take water back into the body. It is also home to trillions of helpful bacteria, which break down some of the leftovers and even make vitamins your body can use. After a day or more, whatever your body cannot use leaves the body. From lunch to the end, the whole trip usually takes one to three days. You can help your digestion every day. Chew slowly, drink water, and eat fiber, which you get from vegetables, fruit, beans, and whole grains. Fiber cannot be digested, but it keeps food moving smoothly and feeds your helpful gut bacteria. Now look at the clock. What time did you eat lunch today? Type it into the model and find out where your lunch is right now.": "935c78594bdd",
